    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let $A = (A,0),$ $B = (0,B),$ and $C = (C,0)$ be the points of tangency of the ellipse with the $x$-axis, tangent to the $y$-axis, and the $y$-axis, respectively.\n\n[asy]\n\nunitsize(0.4 cm);\n\npair O, A, B, C, F;\n\nO = (-6,1);\n\nA = (8,2);\n\nB = (8,1);\n\nC = (7,1);\n\nF = rotate(90)*(7,1);\n\ndraw((-7,0)--(5,0));\ndraw((0,-1)--(0,3));\ndraw(C--A);\ndraw(B--C);\ndraw(A--B);\n\nlabel(\"$C$\", C, NW);\nlabel(\"$K$\", F, NW);\nfield(C);\ndraw(C--F,Arrow(6));\nlabel(\"$K$\", F, N, fontsize(4));\ndraw(B--O--C,Arrows(5));\nlabel(\"$A$\", A, W);\nlabel(\"$B$\", B, Euclidean);\napadphantom(7); plotgraph((-3,2.5));\n\n[/asy]\n\nSince the axes are parallel to the coordinate axes, the equation of the circle in these forms becomes $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4.$ Note that the center of the circle is $A = (4,0)$ and $B = (0,1).$ $C = (C,0)$ is the center of the ellipse. The distance between its centers at $A$ and $C$ is the minor axis of the ellipse. The distance between the centers at $B$ and $C$ is the major axis of the ellipse.\n\nBy the tangent properties of the ellipse and its relationship to the circle, the points of tangency satisfy the following conditions:\n\n$A^{2} + 0^{2} = 4$, since $A = (4,0).$\n\n$1^{2} + 0^{2} = 8;$\n\n$C^{2} + 0^{2} = 1.$\n\nAssumptions and conclusions for these equations are:\n\n$A^{2} = 4;$\n\n$C^{2} = 8 - 1 = 7;$\n\nand $B^{2} = 1.$\n\nThere is also the equation:\n\n$B^{2} + 1^{2} = 6.$\n\nThe above assumptions and conclusions can be quantified, thus yielding:\n\n$x^{2} + y^{2} = 8 - 2x + 1;$\n\n$(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} = 8),$ and\n\n$y^{2} = 6.$\n\nThe length of the semiconic axis is $\\sqrt5$ from the $x$-axis at $C,$ and the length of the focal dials from the $x$-axis at $A$ is $\\sqrt5.$ This same length applies to the diameter of the circle relative to the major axis at $B.$ We begin with the formula $b^{2} = a^{2} - c^{2}.$ In this equation, we find:\n\n$\\sqrt5 = 6 - c^{2};$\n\n$c^{2} = 6 - \\sqrt5;$\n\n$c = \\sqrt{6 - \\sqrt5}.$\n\nWe now use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the distance between the foci of the ellipse. For an ellipse, the distance between its foci is $2c;$ so the distance between the foci of the ellipse is $2c.$ We now determine the distance between the foci of the ellipse:\n\n$2c = 2\\sqrt{6 - \\sqrt5}.$\n\nThus, the foci of the ellipse are located at $(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt6 - 2\\sqrt5),$\n\nand the distance between the foci is $\\boxed{2\\sqrt{6 - \\sqrt5}}.$. The answer is 2\\sqrt{6-\\sqrt5}",
